TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Launch","issue":"The report names Vega E for a 2025 launch even though it states Vega E's first launch is planned for 2024; the schedule and launcher availability are speculative. It does not establish a committed rideshare or demonstrate that a compatible 600 km SSO injection is available."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The RAAN value is not determined by the launch facility's location. Launch latitude constrains achievable inclination, while RAAN at injection depends on launch time and the desired orbital plane."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A circular orbit has undefined argument of perigee and true anomaly, but the argument of latitude is not an independent classical element that resolves both generally; specifying argument of latitude is sufficient for the spacecraft's position in this circular orbit, with epoch and RAAN convention still needed for a reproducible state."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The claim that access frequency is maximized by a ground station close to the poles is not generally valid for a single station; access depends on station latitude, orbit geometry, elevation mask, and the ground track. The reported passes are not reconciled with the claim of near-constant contact."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"A polar/near-polar orbit does not maintain significant contact for the vast majority of passes at a station; contact duration depends on elevation limits and geometry. The report gives durations but no elevation mask or link-budget/data-volume demonstration."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A drag sail is a plausible passive disposal concept at 600 km, but no sail area, spacecraft mass, predicted decay time, or compliance analysis is provided. The discussion also describes an uncontrolled descent as an option without establishing its acceptability under applicable debris-mitigation guidance."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Wikipedia is used as a source despite the task explicitly stating that Wikipedia is not acceptable; most entries also lack complete bibliographic details and access dates."}],"overall":"A relevant Australian reef-monitoring concept is supported by useful STK imagery and access data, but the design remains preliminary. Launcher feasibility, orbit definition, ground-access rationale, and disposal analysis need stronger technical substantiation; report referencing and presentation also require improvement.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report explains the reef-monitoring mission, its environmental rationale, and why one spacecraft is considered sufficient. It identifies likely agency users and public funding, but does not clearly distinguish the spacecraft operator from its customers or provide detailed user roles.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report includes substantial STK-derived orbit and access material, orbital elements, a selected launch site and vehicle, ground facility, mission duration, and a disposal concept. However, the launch plan remains speculative, the maneuver and EOL discussion is not fully engineered, and some orbit/ground-access reasoning is inaccurate. Screenshots are supplied, but no relevant software files are included.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarizes the principal design choices and acknowledges uncertainty about the proposed launcher. It is relevant but largely recaps the report and does not draw detailed conclusions from the analysis or address its limitations.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"After analysis of each mission segment, a fully described satellite configuration has been constructed."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable structure, executive summary, figures, tables, and bibliography. However, figure/table presentation and labelling are inconsistent, references are incomplete and include Wikipedia despite the task excluding it, and prose contains noticeable grammar and wording errors. These issues reduce the report's professional polish.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
